From: mpc-pi
Subject: [PATCH] pipewire: declare a stream latency, and stop hand-rolling the buffering PipeWire does

THE DEFECT: the stream was created without PW_KEY_NODE_LATENCY, so it inherited
the graph default - one quantum, 64/44100, 1.45ms, no slack at all - and the
emulation thread had to land every 1.45ms window or the ring came up short.

Isolated by playing MAME's own recording through pw-play WHILE MAME was running:
same graph, same device, same 64-frame quantum, same instant, both audible
together - pw-play perfectly clean, MAME crackling. Nothing shared can be at
fault when two streams sharing all of it behave differently. The difference is
in pw-dump:

    :speaker    node.latency 64/44100      (inherited default)
    pw-play     node.latency 4410/44100    (declared, 100ms)

MAME now declares MAME_PIPEWIRE_NODE_LATENCY frames (default 1024). This does
NOT change input latency, which is set by the graph quantum; it only says how
far ahead this output stream may run - which is the buffering m_prebuffer_frames
has been trying to imitate inside the callback all along.

The rest of this patch is the instrumentation and the smaller faults found while
narrowing to it, all of which are real and none of which were the cause:

  * pw_stream_connect() passed the format alone with no SPA_PARAM_Buffers, so
    the pool was PipeWire's default; when it ran dry the callback returned
    without queueing, silently dropping a cycle. Now requests
    MAME_PIPEWIRE_BUFFERS (default 8) and counts dequeue failures.
  * buffer->requested may legitimately be 0, and every use of it was unguarded -
    a zero request would memset nothing, fetch nothing, and queue a zero-length
    chunk. Now clamped to the buffer capacity, with the zero case counted.
  * A single short callback re-armed prebuffering, replacing following callbacks
    with memset(0) until the cushion refilled - one late frame became whole
    buffers of digital silence. Now gated on MAME_PIPEWIRE_REARM_AFTER.
  * The output cushion was one quantum plus one producer update (96 frames at
    q64). Now MAME_PIPEWIRE_PREBUFFER_QUANTA quanta.
  * The pacing wait was unbounded and never slept: it hung the emulation thread
    outright when the consumer stalled - at a 256-frame quantum the machine
    froze on its boot screen with systemd still reporting the service active -
    and otherwise spun a core. Bounded by MAME_PIPEWIRE_PACE_BUDGET_US.
  * m_prebuffer_frames and m_producer_frames are running maxima that only grow;
    restarting pacing now resets them.
  * MAME_PIPEWIRE_LATE_US makes the late-update threshold settable.

Measurements that led here, for the next reader: the emulator's own -wavwrite
render is clean and plays perfectly through the same DAC; the ring reports 0
underruns and 0 overruns in the failing configuration; the device reports 0
xruns; and steady-state production is 44,083 frames/s, 100.0% of realtime. See
docs/audio-chain.md for the stage-by-stage map and the metrics that lie.
